<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Uzbekistan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Uzbekistan is a country in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> covering much of historic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a>, including the cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Tashkent</a>. It was the heartland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurid Empire</a>, whose capital was Samarkand, and an important part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khanate">Chagatai Empire</a>. Many of the region's great medieval monuments date from the Timurid period.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/uzbekistan.jpg" alt="Uzbekistan" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Farhod Yoʻldoshev · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Uzbekistan.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The city of Qarshi in <b>Uzbekistan</b> takes its name from the palace that Kebek Khan built there, near Nasaf, in the early 14th century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chagatai-empire">Chagatai Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Their stronghold was Tokharistan, on the northern slopes of the Hindu Kush in today's southern <b>Uzbekistan</b> and northern Afghanistan, with a capital probably at Kunduz.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hephthalite-empire">Hephthalite Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>He rose from the minor nobility of the Barlas tribe in Transoxiana (modern <b>Uzbekistan</b>), within the Chagatai Khanate, through military talent, political ruthlessness and skilful use of the Mongol framework of legitimacy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/timurid-empire">Timurid Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Their early coinage imitated Greco-Bactrian issues, and at Khalchayan in southern <b>Uzbekistan</b> a palace or hall was decorated with painted clay sculptures of nomad aristocrats in Hellenistic style.</blockquote><a href="/empire/yuezhi">Yuezhi · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 20th century Turkey adopted the Latin alphabet in 1928, the Turkic peoples of the Soviet Union were made to write in Cyrillic, and after 1991 several independent states, such as Azerbaijan, <b>Uzbekistan</b> and Turkmenistan, moved to Latin alphabets, as Kazakhstan has begun to do.</blockquote><a href="/language/old-turkic">Old Turkic · Change &amp; Descendants</a></li><li><blockquote>Today it is the official language of Iran, an official language of Afghanistan, as Dari, and of Tajikistan, as Tajik, and is spoken by large communities in <b>Uzbekistan</b> and by a worldwide diaspora.</blockquote><a href="/language/persian">Persian · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li></ul></section>
